From 398d72a4d51bdd5a157ee058d0c0f0213afd0be0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cycles Houdini engine authoring <engine@localhost>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 19:10:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Share the OptiX curve build input between sub-devices

With several GPUs, MultiDevice::build_bvh builds every bottom level
acceleration structure once per sub-device, and each OptiX sub-device
computed the same curve build input (ribbon bounds, or thick and linear
curve vertices and indices) again. The work grew with the number of GPUs.

MultiDevice::build_bvh now owns a BVHSharedBuild for the duration of one
call and points each sub-device BVH at it only while that sub-device builds
(reset on every exit path). The first OptiX sub-device computes the input
into host arrays under the mutex; the others reuse it when geometry, curve
shape, motion steps, segment and vertex counts match, and otherwise compute
their own without touching the shared arrays. The arrays are never written
after they are published, so readers need no lock, and they are released
when the multi-device build of that geometry returns. Each device uploads
from the shared arrays into device-only memory that may fall back to
mapped host memory, like the read-only vectors it replaces. A single
device, and devices of other types, never see the pointer and build exactly
as before. The fill itself moved unchanged into fill_curve_build_input.

Rationale: The curve build input was recomputed per GPU although it is identical; computing it once keeps the work independent of the GPU count and guarantees every device builds from the same input.
Contract: Every OptiX sub-device receives the same AABB, index and vertex arrays as before for ribbon, thick and linear curves and all motion steps; single-device builds are unchanged and no shared data outlives one multi-device build.
Tests: tests/houdini_curve_subdivisions_smoke.py, tests/houdini_hair_smoke.py, tests/houdini_rls_hair_smoke.py
